{{!-- ── PERMIT CATEGORIES ──────────────────────────────────────────── --}}
<section class="hp-section hp-categories hp-reveal">
    <div class="container">
        <div class="hp-section-header">
            <h2 class="hp-section-title">What Permits Do You Need?</h2>
            <p class="hp-section-desc">Every construction project, no matter how small, falls under some type of permit requirement. The tricky part is that rules change from one city to the next, and sometimes even between neighborhoods within the same city. Here is a breakdown of the most common permit categories homeowners run into.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hp-categories-grid">
            <a href="/tag/building-permits/" class="hp-cat-card">
                <div class="hp-cat-icon hp-cat-icon--blue">
                    <svg width="28" height="28" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="1" y="4" width="22" height="16" rx="2" ry="2"></rect><polyline points="1 10 12 15 23 10"></polyline></svg>
                </div>
                <h3 class="hp-cat-title">Building Permits</h3>
                <p class="hp-cat-desc">Building permits are the most common type homeowners deal with. You need one for almost anything that changes the structure of your home. Adding a room, knocking down a wall, or even converting a garage into a living space all require a building permit in most jurisdictions.</p>
            </a>
            <a href="/tag/electrical-permits/" class="hp-cat-card">
                <div class="hp-cat-icon hp-cat-icon--amber">
                    <svg width="28" height="28" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><polygon points="13 2 3 14 12 14 11 22 21 10 12 10 13 2"></polygon></svg>
                </div>
                <h3 class="hp-cat-title">Electrical Permits</h3>
                <p class="hp-cat-desc">If you are planning to add new circuits, upgrade your electrical panel, or install outdoor lighting, an electrical permit is almost always required. Many homeowners do not realize that even replacing a breaker box or adding an outlet in a new location needs city approval.</p>
            </a>
            <a href="/tag/plumbing-permits/" class="hp-cat-card">
                <div class="hp-cat-icon hp-cat-icon--cyan">
                    <svg width="28" height="28" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 2L2 7l10 5 10-5-10-5z"></path><path d="M2 17l10 5 10-5"></path><path d="M2 12l10 5 10-5"></path></svg>
                </div>
                <h3 class="hp-cat-title">Plumbing Permits</h3>
                <p class="hp-cat-desc">Moving a sink, adding a bathroom, or running new water lines all require plumbing permits. Cities want to make sure your water connections meet code and that your drainage plans will not cause issues for the municipal sewer system.</p>
            </a>
            <a href="/tag/zoning-laws/" class="hp-cat-card">
                <div class="hp-cat-icon hp-cat-icon--green">
                    <svg width="28" height="28" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M21 10c0 7-9 13-9 13s-9-6-9-13a9 9 0 0 1 18 0z"></path><circle cx="12" cy="10" r="3"></circle></svg>
                </div>
                <h3 class="hp-cat-title">Zoning &amp; Land Use</h3>
                <p class="hp-cat-desc">Zoning laws determine what you can and cannot do with your property. Want to build a detached garage? Run a home business? Add a second story? Zoning regulations dictate all of this, and violating them can result in fines or being forced to tear down your work.</p>
            </a>
            <a href="/tag/hvac-permits/" class="hp-cat-card">
                <div class="hp-cat-icon hp-cat-icon--red">
                    <svg width="28" height="28" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M14 14.76V3.5a2.5 2.5 0 0 0-5 0v11.26a4.5 4.5 0 1 0 5 0z"></path></svg>
                </div>
                <h3 class="hp-cat-title">HVAC Permits</h3>
                <p class="hp-cat-desc">Installing a new air conditioning system, replacing a furnace, or adding ductwork all need HVAC permits. These permits exist because improper installation can lead to carbon monoxide leaks, poor ventilation, and serious health risks for your family.</p>
            </a>
            <a href="/tag/roofing-permits/" class="hp-cat-card">
                <div class="hp-cat-icon hp-cat-icon--purple">
                    <svg width="28" height="28" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M3 21h18"></path><path d="M5 21V7l7-4 7 4v14"></path><path d="M9 21v-6h6v6"></path></svg>
                </div>
                <h3 class="hp-cat-title">Roofing &amp; Siding</h3>
                <p class="hp-cat-desc">Most cities require a permit for full roof replacements. Some even require permits for re-roofing over an existing layer. Siding replacement follows similar rules in many municipalities, especially when you are changing the exterior material type.</p>
            </a>
            <a href="/tag/deck-permits/" class="hp-cat-card">
                <div class="hp-cat-icon hp-cat-icon--teal">
                    <svg width="28" height="28" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="3" y="8" width="18" height="12" rx="2"></rect><path d="M7 8V6a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h6a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v2"></path><line x1="12" y1="12" x2="12" y2="16"></line></svg>
                </div>
                <h3 class="hp-cat-title">Deck &amp; Patio Permits</h3>
                <p class="hp-cat-desc">Building a deck seems simple, but most cities require a permit if the deck is above a certain height or if it will be attached to your house. The permit process usually requires a site plan showing the deck dimensions and distance from property lines.</p>
            </a>
            <a href="/tag/renovation-permits/" class="hp-cat-card">
                <div class="hp-cat-icon hp-cat-icon--orange">
                    <svg width="28" height="28" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M14.7 6.3a1 1 0 0 0 0 1.4l1.6 1.6a1 1 0 0 0 1.4 0l3.77-3.77a6 6 0 0 1-7.94 7.94l-6.91 6.91a2.12 2.12 0 0 1-3-3l6.91-6.91a6 6 0 0 1 7.94-7.94l-3.76 3.76z"></path></svg>
                </div>
                <h3 class="hp-cat-title">Renovation Permits</h3>
                <p class="hp-cat-desc">Kitchen remodels, bathroom renovations, and basement finishings often need permits, especially if you are moving plumbing or electrical. Even cosmetic changes like removing a load-bearing wall require structural review and approval before work begins.</p>
            </a>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

{{!-- ── HOW TO GET A PERMIT ────────────────────────────────────────── --}}
<section class="hp-section hp-steps-section hp-reveal">
    <div class="container">
        <div class="hp-section-header">
            <h2 class="hp-section-title">How to Get a Building Permit in 5 Steps</h2>
            <p class="hp-section-desc">The permit process feels intimidating the first time, but it follows the same basic pattern in nearly every city across the country. Here is exactly what you need to do, step by step, from research to final inspection.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hp-steps-grid">
            <div class="hp-step">
                <div class="hp-step-number">1</div>
                <div class="hp-step-content">
                    <h3 class="hp-step-title">Research Your Local Requirements</h3>
                    <p class="hp-step-desc">Before you do anything else, visit your city or county building department website. Look for their permit guidelines, fee schedules, and required documents. Some cities have everything online, while others still require in-person visits. Call the permitting office if you are unsure about your specific project.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="hp-step">
                <div class="hp-step-number">2</div>
                <div class="hp-step-content">
                    <h3 class="hp-step-title">Prepare Your Application &amp; Plans</h3>
                    <p class="hp-step-desc">Most permit applications require detailed plans showing what you intend to build or modify. For simple projects, a hand-drawn sketch might suffice. For larger renovations, you may need architect-drawn blueprints. Include dimensions, materials, and how the work connects to existing systems.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="hp-step">
                <div class="hp-step-number">3</div>
                <div class="hp-step-content">
                    <h3 class="hp-step-title">Submit &amp; Pay Fees</h3>
                    <p class="hp-step-desc">Submit your completed application along with the required plans and permit fees. Fees vary widely depending on the project scope and your location. A simple deck permit might cost $100, while a full home addition could run into thousands. Many cities now accept online submissions.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="hp-step">
                <div class="hp-step-number">4</div>
                <div class="hp-step-content">
                    <h3 class="hp-step-title">Schedule Inspections</h3>
                    <p class="hp-step-desc">Once your permit is approved, you can start work. However, you will need to schedule inspections at various stages. Common inspection points include foundation, framing, electrical rough-in, plumbing rough-in, and final inspection. Never skip an inspection, as it can cause problems later.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="hp-step">
                <div class="hp-step-number">5</div>
                <div class="hp-step-content">
                    <h3 class="hp-step-title">Get Final Approval &amp; Keep Records</h3>
                    <p class="hp-step-desc">After the final inspection passes, you will receive a certificate of occupancy or completion. Keep this document along with all your permit paperwork in a safe place. You will need these records when you sell your home, as buyers and their inspectors will ask for proof that work was permitted.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

