{{!-- ── COMMON MISTAKES ────────────────────────────────────────────── --}}
<section class="hp-section hp-mistakes-section hp-reveal">
    <div class="container">
        <div class="hp-section-header">
            <h2 class="hp-section-title">Common Permit Mistakes Homeowners Make</h2>
            <p class="hp-section-desc">We see the same mistakes over and over again. These errors can cost you thousands of dollars in fines, force you to undo completed work, or create serious safety hazards. Here are the most common ones and how to avoid them.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hp-mistakes-grid">
            <div class="hp-mistake-card">
                <div class="hp-mistake-icon">
                    <svg width="22" height="22" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"></circle><line x1="15" y1="9" x2="9" y2="15"></line><line x1="9" y1="9" x2="15" y2="15"></line></svg>
                </div>
                <h3 class="hp-mistake-title">Skipping Permits Entirely</h3>
                <p class="hp-mistake-desc">This is by far the biggest mistake. Homeowners often think small projects do not need permits, but the definition of "permit-required work" is broader than most people realize. Fines for unpermitted work can range from $500 to $25,000 depending on your city.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="hp-mistake-card">
                <div class="hp-mistake-icon">
                    <svg width="22" height="22" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M10.29 3.86L1.82 18a2 2 0 0 0 1.71 3h16.94a2 2 0 0 0 1.71-3L13.71 3.86a2 2 0 0 0-3.42 0z"></path><line x1="12" y1="9" x2="12" y2="13"></line><line x1="12" y1="17" x2="12.01" y2="17"></line></svg>
                </div>
                <h3 class="hp-mistake-title">Starting Work Before Approval</h3>
                <p class="hp-mistake-desc">Some homeowners submit a permit application and immediately start demolition or construction. If your permit gets denied, you may have to restore everything to its original condition at your own expense. Always wait for written approval.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="hp-mistake-card">
                <div class="hp-mistake-icon">
                    <svg width="22" height="22" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"></circle><polyline points="12 6 12 12 16 14"></polyline></svg>
                </div>
                <h3 class="hp-mistake-title">Missing Inspection Deadlines</h3>
                <p class="hp-mistake-desc">Permits have expiration dates, usually 6 to 12 months. If you miss inspections or let the permit lapse, you may have to start the entire process over. Schedule inspections promptly at each stage of your project and keep track of deadlines.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="hp-mistake-card">
                <div class="hp-mistake-icon">
                    <svg width="22" height="22" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="3" y="3" width="18" height="18" rx="2" ry="2"></rect><line x1="9" y1="3" x2="9" y2="21"></line></svg>
                </div>
                <h3 class="hp-mistake-title">Ignoring Zoning Setbacks</h3>
                <p class="hp-mistake-desc">Every property has setback requirements that dictate how close you can build to property lines, streets, and other structures. Ignoring these rules is one of the most common reasons permits get denied. Always check setback rules before designing your project.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="hp-mistake-card">
                <div class="hp-mistake-icon">
                    <svg width="22" height="22" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M17 21v-2a4 4 0 0 0-4-4H5a4 4 0 0 0-4 4v2"></path><circle cx="9" cy="7" r="4"></circle><path d="M23 21v-2a4 4 0 0 0-3-3.87"></path><path d="M16 3.13a4 4 0 0 1 0 7.75"></path></svg>
                </div>
                <h3 class="hp-mistake-title">Hiring Unlicensed Contractors</h3>
                <p class="hp-mistake-desc">In most states, unlicensed contractors cannot legally pull permits on your behalf. If something goes wrong, you have very little recourse. Always verify a contractor's license number with your state licensing board before signing any agreement.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="hp-mistake-card">
                <div class="hp-mistake-icon">
                    <svg width="22" height="22" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M14 2H6a2 2 0 0 0-2 2v16a2 2 0 0 0 2 2h12a2 2 0 0 0 2-2V8z"></path><polyline points="14 2 14 8 20 8"></polyline></svg>
                </div>
                <h3 class="hp-mistake-title">Not Keeping Permit Records</h3>
                <p class="hp-mistake-desc">When it comes time to sell your house, the buyer's home inspector will ask for permit records on any modifications. Without them, you may face delays in closing or be forced to get retroactive permits, which can be complicated and expensive.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

{{!-- ── PERMIT COSTS GUIDE ────────────────────────────────────────── --}}
<section class="hp-section hp-costs-section hp-reveal">
    <div class="container">
        <div class="hp-section-header">
            <h2 class="hp-section-title">Understanding Permit Costs</h2>
            <p class="hp-section-desc">One of the first questions every homeowner asks is how much a permit will cost. The answer depends on several factors, including where you live, the type of work, and the total value of the project. Here is a realistic breakdown so you can budget properly.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hp-costs-grid">
            <div class="hp-cost-card">
                <div class="hp-cost-header">
                    <h3 class="hp-cost-type">Small Projects</h3>
                    <span class="hp-cost-range">$50 - $300</span>
                </div>
                <p class="hp-cost-desc">Minor repairs like replacing a water heater, re-roofing, or adding an outlet typically fall in this range. These permits are usually processed quickly, often within a few business days. Some cities even offer same-day over-the-counter permits for straightforward residential work.</p>
                <ul class="hp-cost-list">
                    <li>Water heater replacement</li>
                    <li>Re-roofing (same material)</li>
                    <li>Window replacement</li>
                    <li>Fence installation</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
            <div class="hp-cost-card hp-cost-card--featured">
                <div class="hp-cost-badge">Most Common</div>
                <div class="hp-cost-header">
                    <h3 class="hp-cost-type">Medium Projects</h3>
                    <span class="hp-cost-range">$300 - $1,500</span>
                </div>
                <p class="hp-cost-desc">Room additions, deck construction, and kitchen remodels with plumbing or electrical changes fall into this bracket. These require plan review and multiple inspections. Processing usually takes two to four weeks depending on your city's backlog and the complexity of your plans.</p>
                <ul class="hp-cost-list">
                    <li>Room addition</li>
                    <li>Deck construction</li>
                    <li>Kitchen remodel</li>
                    <li>Bathroom addition</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
            <div class="hp-cost-card">
