            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

{{!-- ── KNOW YOUR RIGHTS ───────────────────────────────────────────── --}}
<section class="hp-section hp-rights-section hp-reveal">
    <div class="container">
        <div class="hp-section-header">
            <h2 class="hp-section-title">Know Your Rights as a Homeowner</h2>
            <p class="hp-section-desc">Understanding your rights when dealing with building departments and contractors can save you from expensive problems. Many homeowners do not realize they have significant protections under state and local laws.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hp-rights-grid">
            <div class="hp-right-card">
                <div class="hp-right-icon">
                    <svg width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 22s8-4 8-10V5l-8-3-8 3v7c0 6 8 10 8 10z"></path></svg>
                </div>
                <h3 class="hp-right-title">Right to Appeal a Denial</h3>
                <p class="hp-right-desc">If your permit application gets denied, you have the right to know exactly why. Every denial must be based on specific code violations or zoning rules. You can appeal the decision to a zoning board or building board of appeals in most jurisdictions. Many denials are resolved by making minor modifications to your plans.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="hp-right-card">
                <div class="hp-right-icon">
                    <svg width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="3" y="11" width="18" height="11" rx="2" ry="2"></rect><path d="M7 11V7a5 5 0 0 1 10 0v4"></path></svg>
                </div>
                <h3 class="hp-right-title">Protection Against Contractor Fraud</h3>
                <p class="hp-right-desc">Most states require contractors to carry a license, insurance, and a written contract for projects above a certain dollar amount. You have the right to a three-day cooling-off period for contracts signed at your home. If a contractor abandons your project, you can file a complaint with the state licensing board.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="hp-right-card">
                <div class="hp-right-icon">
                    <svg width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"></circle><polyline points="12 6 12 12 16 14"></polyline></svg>
                </div>
                <h3 class="hp-right-title">Reasonable Timeline Expectations</h3>
                <p class="hp-right-desc">Building departments are required to process permit applications within a reasonable timeframe. Most states have laws that set maximum review periods. Simple permits should be processed in days, not weeks. If your city is consistently taking months, you may have grounds to escalate the matter.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="hp-right-card">
                <div class="hp-right-icon">
                    <svg width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M14 2H6a2 2 0 0 0-2 2v16a2 2 0 0 0 2 2h12a2 2 0 0 0 2-2V8z"></path><polyline points="14 2 14 8 20 8"></polyline><line x1="16" y1="13" x2="8" y2="13"></line><line x1="16" y1="17" x2="8" y2="17"></line></svg>
                </div>
                <h3 class="hp-right-title">Access to Public Records</h3>
                <p class="hp-right-desc">Building permits, inspection reports, and code enforcement records are public information in most states. You can look up permits on any property, including your neighbors'. This is useful when you are buying a home and want to verify that previous renovations were done legally and inspected properly.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

{{!-- ── FAQ SECTION ────────────────────────────────────────────────── --}}
<section class="hp-section hp-faq-section hp-reveal">
    <div class="container">
        <div class="hp-section-header">
            <h2 class="hp-section-title">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
            <p class="hp-section-desc">These are the questions homeowners ask us most often. If your question is not answered here, use the search bar above or browse our guides by city and permit type.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hp-faq-list">
            <details class="hp-faq-item">
                <summary class="hp-faq-question">Do I really need a permit for a small renovation?</summary>
                <div class="hp-faq-answer">
                    <p>It depends on your city, but in most cases, yes. Even seemingly minor work like moving a plumbing fixture, adding an electrical circuit, or removing a non-load-bearing wall often requires a permit. The general rule is this: if the work changes the structure, systems, or safety of your home, you probably need a permit. Painting, flooring, and cosmetic updates usually do not require one. Always check with your local building department before starting any project.</p>
                </div>
            </details>
            <details class="hp-faq-item">
                <summary class="hp-faq-question">What happens if I build without a permit?</summary>
                <div class="hp-faq-answer">
                    <p>The consequences vary by city but can be severe. Common penalties include fines ranging from $500 to $25,000, a stop-work order that halts your project immediately, and a requirement to tear down or undo the unpermitted work. Some cities also impose daily fines that add up quickly. When you sell your home, unpermitted work must be disclosed, and it can significantly reduce your property value or scare away buyers entirely.</p>
                </div>
            </details>
            <details class="hp-faq-item">
                <summary class="hp-faq-question">How long does it take to get a permit approved?</summary>
                <div class="hp-faq-answer">
                    <p>Timeline varies based on the project and location. Simple permits like re-roofing or HVAC replacement can often be approved same-day or within a few business days. Medium projects like room additions or kitchen remodels typically take two to four weeks for plan review. Large projects requiring structural engineering review can take four to eight weeks. Some cities offer expedited review for an additional fee if you are working under a tight deadline.</p>
                </div>
            </details>
            <details class="hp-faq-item">
                <summary class="hp-faq-question">Can I pull a permit myself or does my contractor do it?</summary>
                <div class="hp-faq-answer">
                    <p>In most cities, homeowners can pull their own permits for work on their primary residence. However, some types of work require a licensed contractor to pull the permit. Electrical and plumbing permits often fall into this category depending on your state. When hiring a contractor, make sure the contract clearly states who is responsible for obtaining permits. A reputable contractor will handle the permit process as part of their service.</p>
                </div>
            </details>
            <details class="hp-faq-item">
                <summary class="hp-faq-question">Do I need a permit for a shed or storage building?</summary>
                <div class="hp-faq-answer">
                    <p>In many cities, small sheds under 120 square feet do not require a building permit. However, rules vary widely. Some cities exempt sheds under 200 square feet, while others require permits for any permanent structure regardless of size. If the shed will have electricity or plumbing, you will definitely need separate permits for those systems. Always check your local zoning ordinance before building.</p>
                </div>
            </details>
            <details class="hp-faq-item">
                <summary class="hp-faq-question">What is the difference between a building permit and zoning approval?</summary>
                <div class="hp-faq-answer">
                    <p>A building permit focuses on the physical construction: structural integrity, electrical safety, plumbing code compliance, and fire safety. Zoning approval, on the other hand, deals with how the property is used and what can be built on it. Zoning rules cover things like setback distances, building height limits, property use categories, and lot coverage ratios. You typically need both for any significant project.</p>
                </div>
            </details>
            <details class="hp-faq-item">
                <summary class="hp-faq-question">Will a permit increase my property taxes?</summary>
                <div class="hp-faq-answer">
                    <p>Possibly, but usually the increase is modest and reflects the actual increase in your home's value. When you add a room, finish a basement, or build an addition, the improved space increases your property's assessed value. This is generally a good thing because it means your investment is recognized. The tax increase is typically a small fraction of the value added. Cosmetic improvements like painting or new flooring usually do not affect your tax assessment.</p>
                </div>
            </details>
            <details class="hp-faq-item">
                <summary class="hp-faq-question">Can a homeowner do their own electrical or plumbing work?</summary>
                <div class="hp-faq-answer">
                    <p>This varies significantly by state and city. Some jurisdictions allow homeowners to perform their own electrical and plumbing work on their primary residence if they live there and pass a homeowner's exam. Others require all electrical and plumbing work to be performed by licensed professionals. Even when homeowners are allowed to do the work themselves, the work must still pass inspection. We recommend checking your local regulations before attempting any DIY electrical or plumbing projects.</p>
                </div>
            </details>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

